这就像个小窍门。

1871
01:22:20.880 --> 01:22:21.400
It is.
确实是。

1872
01:22:21.540 --> 01:22:23.860
You don't have to melt a bag of marshmallows.
你不用非得去融化一整袋棉花糖。

1873
01:22:24.580 --> 01:22:35.780
The sandwich made with peanut butter and marshmallow cream continued to be eaten but was not called a fluffernutter until 1960 when an advertising firm that was working with this company,
这种用花生酱和棉花糖酱做的三明治一直有人吃，但直到1960年才被称为“棉花糖花生酱三明治”，当时一家与该公司合作的广告公司，

1874
01:22:36.000 --> 01:22:41.480
Durkee Mower, that owns marshmallow cream, thought it might be a more effective way to market the sandwich.
也就是拥有棉花糖酱品牌的德基·莫尔公司，认为这可能是推广这种三明治更有效的方式。

1875
01:22:41.480 --> 01:22:47.060
The sandwich has close ties to New England and Massachusetts, particularly Somerville.
这种三明治与新英格兰地区，特别是萨默维尔有着密切的联系。

1876
01:22:47.300 --> 01:22:53.000
In fact, Somerville holds an annual festival called What the Fluff?
事实上，萨默维尔每年都会举办一个名为“棉花糖是什么？”的节日。

1877
01:22:53.500 --> 01:22:53.900
Oh.
噢。

1878
01:22:54.320 --> 01:22:54.560
Okay.
好吧。

1879
01:22:54.780 --> 01:22:58.040
Got anything nasty you want to say about their festival, Jenna?
珍娜，你有什么关于他们节日的坏话想说吗？

1880
01:22:58.380 --> 01:22:58.840
No.
没有。

1881
01:22:59.220 --> 01:23:00.040
Sam is giggling.
萨姆在咯咯笑。

1882
01:23:00.500 --> 01:23:06.920
The festival incorporates music, visual arts, games, and a cooking contest based around fluff and fluffernutters.
这个节日包含了音乐、视觉艺术、游戏以及以棉花糖和棉花糖花生酱三明治为主题的烹饪比赛。

1883
01:23:06.920 --> 01:23:12.120
And October 8th is National Fluffernutter Day.
而且10月8日是全国棉花糖花生酱三明治日。

1884
01:23:13.160 --> 01:23:13.840
Wow.
哇。

1885
01:23:14.320 --> 01:23:15.280
There you go.
就是这样。

1886
01:23:15.740 --> 01:23:17.260
Here's what I'm wondering.
我好奇的是这个。

1887
01:23:17.540 --> 01:23:17.920
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

1888
01:23:18.340 --> 01:23:22.060
When did the term fluffer come around?
“fluffer”这个词是什么时候出现的？

1889
01:23:22.060 --> 01:23:29.820
Does it predate or postdate the advertising agency's naming of this sandwich?
它是在广告公司给这个三明治命名之前还是之后？

1890
01:23:30.240 --> 01:23:31.800
Well, it was named in the 60s.
嗯，它是在60年代命名的。

1891
01:23:31.860 --> 01:23:33.640
I'll let you go down that rabbit hole.
我就让你自己去钻那个牛角尖吧。

1892
01:23:33.800 --> 01:23:35.820
I'll let you Google fluffer origin.
我就让你自己去谷歌搜索“fluffer”的起源吧。

1893
01:23:35.820 --> 01:23:37.060
Because I'm curious.
因为我很好奇。

1894
01:23:37.080 --> 01:23:38.540
I mean, you know.
我是说，你知道的。

1895
01:23:38.820 --> 01:23:39.160
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

1896
01:23:39.320 --> 01:23:43.880
I feel like fluffers were around in the 70s?
我觉得70年代就有“fluffer”这个词了？

1897
01:23:45.980 --> 01:23:48.420
So maybe fluffernutter predates it.
所以也许棉花糖花生酱三明治的命名更早。

1898
01:23:48.560 --> 01:23:55.140
So it's just it's an unfortunate thing for the sandwich that there's another maybe meaning.
所以对这个三明治来说，有另一个可能的意思确实挺不幸的。

1899
01:23:55.280 --> 01:23:56.400
And for the record, I'm giggling.
顺便说一句，我刚才在咯咯笑。

1900
01:23:56.620 --> 01:23:58.780
You were familiar with a fluffernutter, right?
你以前听说过棉花糖花生酱三明治，对吧？

1901
01:23:58.800 --> 01:23:59.720
This is something you knew?
这是你早就知道的吗？

1902
01:23:59.860 --> 01:24:00.380
The sandwich.
三明治那个。

1903
01:24:00.620 --> 01:24:00.780
Yeah.
是啊。

1904
01:24:00.920 --> 01:24:02.820
But you were not familiar with a bagel.
但你以前不知道贝果。

1905
01:24:03.040 --> 01:24:06.460
I was by this time.
到那时候我就知道了。

1906
01:24:06.460 --> 01:24:08.020
For the record, that's why I'm giggling.
郑重声明，我刚才在偷笑就是因为这个。

1907
01:24:08.260 --> 01:24:08.460
No, no, no, no.
不，不，不，不。

1908
01:24:08.580 --> 01:24:13.240
For the record, I didn't know what a fluffernutter or a bagel was at all.
郑重声明，我以前根本不知道什么是棉花糖花生酱三明治，也不知道什么是贝果。

1909
01:24:13.380 --> 01:24:16.500
They were all East Coast things, and I didn't know about them.
那些都是东海岸的东西，我以前没听说过。

1910
01:24:17.480 --> 01:24:21.100
I found out about a fluffernutter after I knew what a bagel was.
我是先知道贝果之后，才听说棉花糖花生酱三明治的。

1911
01:24:21.180 --> 01:24:22.180
Got it, got it, got it, got it.
懂了，懂了，懂了，懂了。

1912
01:24:22.180 --> 01:24:31.220
I would love a segment where you just reveal foods that you came to know later in life.
我真想看一个环节，专门让你揭露那些你后来才知道的食物。

1913
01:24:31.420 --> 01:24:35.100
Well, clearly, you're coming to know a fluffernutter later in life.
好吧，显然你现在才刚开始了解棉花糖花生酱三明治。

1914
01:24:35.220 --> 01:24:35.760
I am.
确实。

1915
01:24:35.900 --> 01:24:38.480
I didn't know about this sandwich.
我以前不知道这种三明治。

1916
01:24:38.740 --> 01:24:40.780
I knew about a fluffernutter before you did.
我比你先知道棉花糖花生酱三明治。

1917
01:24:40.940 --> 01:24:42.360
Just put it on record.
记下来，留作记录。

1918
01:24:43.140 --> 01:24:47.220
It sounds maybe gross, but does it taste good?
听起来可能有点恶心，但味道好吗？

1919
01:24:47.220 --> 01:24:47.400
It's so good.
非常好吃。

1920
01:24:47.640 --> 01:24:48.480
It's so good.
真的非常好吃。

1921
01:24:48.680 --> 01:24:50.900
I want to put a sliced banana on it.
我想在上面放点香蕉片。

1922
01:24:51.320 --> 01:24:52.300
Yeah, why not?
好啊，为什么不呢？

1923
01:24:52.520 --> 01:24:52.760
Have you tried that?
你试过吗？

1924
01:24:52.920 --> 01:24:54.720
No, but I'm sure you'd love it.
没试过，但我肯定你会喜欢的。

1925
01:24:54.840 --> 01:24:57.500
I bet at that festival, they deep fry them, too.
我敢打赌在那个节日里，他们还会把这东西拿去油炸。

1926
01:24:58.460 --> 01:24:59.320
At what, the fluff?
什么节日，棉花糖节？

1927
01:24:59.500 --> 01:25:01.500
Yeah, a deep fried fluffernutter.
对，油炸棉花糖花生酱三明治。

1928
01:25:01.980 --> 01:25:03.120
I bet it's good.
我敢说那肯定很好吃。

1929
01:25:04.220 --> 01:25:09.140
Back to the episode, one of my favorite exchanges of dialogue happens in this kitchen.
回到剧集，我最喜欢的对话之一就发生在这个厨房里。

1930
01:25:09.580 --> 01:25:10.020
Okay.
好的。

1931
01:25:11.060 --> 01:25:13.720
Rachel, David Wallace's wife, walks in.
大卫·华莱士的妻子瑞秋走了进来。

1932
01:25:14.060 --> 01:25:14.500
Yes.
没错。

1933
01:25:14.660 --> 01:25:19.100
This says so much about their relationship and what's going on in their marriage right now.
这段对话充分说明了他们的关系，以及他们目前的婚姻状况。

1934
01:25:19.240 --> 01:25:21.160
She goes, hey, honey, how's your day going?
她说，嘿，亲爱的，你今天过得怎么样？

1935
01:25:21.420 --> 01:25:22.680
Did you do anything cool?
做了什么有趣的事吗？

1936
01:25:22.980 --> 01:25:25.560
And he's like, no, sweetie, just sort of hung out.
他回答说，没，甜心，就随便待了待。

1937
01:25:25.960 --> 01:25:28.080
And she says, this is the line.
然后她说，重点来了，就是这句台词。

1938
01:25:28.240 --> 01:25:28.780
This is it.
就是这句。

1939
01:25:28.840 --> 01:25:29.700
I wrote it down as well.
我也把它记下来了。

1940
01:25:29.860 --> 01:25:32.380
Oh, because I saw you had shoes on.
哦，因为我看到你穿了鞋子。

1941
01:25:32.660 --> 01:25:34.720
So I didn't know if you did anything.
所以我不知道你是不是出门做了什么。

1942
01:25:35.480 --> 01:25:35.920
Amazing.
太绝了。

1943
01:25:36.240 --> 01:25:36.500
Amazing.
太绝了。

1944
01:25:37.380 --> 01:25:42.240
Rachel Wallace was played by Jean Villapique.
瑞秋·华莱士由简·维拉皮克饰演。

1945
01:25:42.860 --> 01:25:45.560
She was a member of Second City Improv Group.
她是第二城即兴喜剧团的成员。

1946
01:25:45.940 --> 01:25:47.660
She's done a ton of comedy.
她演过大量的喜剧作品。

1947
01:25:47.940 --> 01:25:52.580
You may have seen her on Veep, Key & Peele, Modern Family, BoJack Horseman.
你可能在《副总统》、《基和皮尔》、《摩登家庭》、《马男波杰克》里见过她。

1948
01:25:53.260 --> 01:25:55.580
She's also a regular on AP Bio.
她还是《生物课》的常驻演员。

1949
01:25:56.240 --> 01:25:59.560
I loved how she clocked the camera as she walked into her kitchen.
我很喜欢她走进厨房时看向镜头的那个瞬间。

1950
01:25:59.900 --> 01:26:00.740
She's walking.
她正走进来。

1951
01:26:00.880 --> 01:26:03.480
She's like, oh, oh, hi, honey.
她当时就像是，噢，噢，嗨，亲爱的。

1952
01:26:03.920 --> 01:26:06.060
She's also so happy to see Michael.
她见到迈克尔也特别高兴。

1953
01:26:06.500 --> 01:26:07.480
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

1954
01:26:07.660 --> 01:26:08.440
What a switch.
真是大反转。

1955
01:26:08.800 --> 01:26:09.640
I know, right?
可不是吗？

1956
01:26:09.980 --> 01:26:15.080
At 14 minutes, 10 seconds, we see Teddy, David Wallace's son, playing the druMs.
在14分10秒处，我们看到大卫·华莱士的儿子泰迪在打鼓。

1957
01:26:15.340 --> 01:26:17.720
He was played by Owen Daniels.
他是由欧文·丹尼尔斯饰演的。

1958
01:26:17.820 --> 01:26:18.780
And you guys remember him.
你们应该还记得他。

1959
01:26:18.840 --> 01:26:21.000
He was in the Cocktails episode in season three.
他曾在第三季的《鸡尾酒会》那一集中出现过。

1960
01:26:21.300 --> 01:26:25.480
Well, I remember talking to Suzanne Daniels about Owen's drumming.
我记得曾和苏珊·丹尼尔斯聊起过欧文打鼓的事。

1961
01:26:26.040 --> 01:26:30.980
And she was just, you know, having some mom pride about what a good drummer he was.
她当时作为母亲，对儿子鼓打得那么好感到非常自豪。

1962
01:26:31.100 --> 01:26:32.600
And then here it got to be on the show.
结果在这里，他真的在剧里露了一手。

1963
01:26:32.740 --> 01:26:33.120
I know.
我知道。

1964
01:26:33.260 --> 01:26:33.680
So cool.
太酷了。

1965
01:26:34.460 --> 01:26:38.460
Well, Michael and David are going to sit in the hot tub.
迈克尔和大卫打算去泡热水澡。

1966
01:26:38.580 --> 01:26:39.660
This is so awkward.
这太尴尬了。

1967
01:26:39.660 --> 01:26:42.260
I mean, he says to his wife, we might go outside.
我是说，他对妻子说，我们可能要出去一下。

1968
01:26:42.860 --> 01:26:43.220
Yeah.
是啊。

1969
01:26:43.460 --> 01:26:48.540
At 16 minutes, 55 seconds, Michael is wearing a t-shirt in the hot tub.
在16分55秒处，迈克尔在热水浴缸里穿着一件T恤。

1970
01:26:49.120 --> 01:26:57.420
And Jen Salata said this was because Steve was in such good shape that they had to hide his physique because it would have been distracting.
詹·萨拉塔说这是因为史蒂夫的身材太好了，他们必须遮住他的体格，否则会让人分心。

1971
01:26:57.740 --> 01:26:58.680
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

1972
01:26:58.680 --> 01:27:02.980
Yeah, that, like, you'd think, how is Michael in such good shape?
是啊，你可能会想，迈克尔怎么会有这么好的身材？

1973
01:27:03.060 --> 01:27:04.580
Michael has, like, a 12-pack.
迈克尔简直有十二块腹肌。

1974
01:27:05.080 --> 01:27:05.280
Yeah.
没错。

1975
01:27:05.540 --> 01:27:12.540
She also said it felt like this really great way to highlight how different David Wallace was being.
她还说，这是一种突出大卫·华莱士反差感的绝佳方式。

1976
01:27:12.640 --> 01:27:16.020
That, like, David Wallace is just shirtless and relaxed.
就像大卫·华莱士那样赤裸着上身，非常放松。

1977
01:27:16.140 --> 01:27:17.040
Drinking a beer.
喝着啤酒。

1978
01:27:17.220 --> 01:27:17.420
Yes.
是的。

1979
01:27:17.440 --> 01:27:18.520
In the middle of the day.
在大白天。

1980
01:27:18.680 --> 01:27:24.220
And Michael would be made, like, it would be more awkward for Michael to be in a t-shirt.
而迈克尔穿着T恤反而会显得更尴尬。

1981
01:27:24.220 --> 01:27:27.200
We got a fan catch from Megan B.
我们收到了来自宾夕法尼亚州波茨维尔的梅根·B发来的粉丝发现。

1982
01:27:27.360 --> 01:27:28.900
in Pottsville, Pennsylvania.
在热水浴缸那场戏里，大卫·华莱士喝的是云岭啤酒。

1983
01:27:29.480 --> 01:27:33.900
In the hot tub scene, David Wallace is drinking a Jungling lager.
而迈克尔喝的是云岭黑啤与淡啤混合酒。

1984
01:27:34.220 --> 01:27:37.880
And Michael is drinking a Jungling black and tan.
来自梅根·B的冷知识。

1985
01:27:38.360 --> 01:27:40.500
Fast fact from Megan B.
云岭是美国最古老的酿酒厂，就始于我老家宾夕法尼亚州的波茨维尔。

1986
01:27:41.520 --> 01:27:48.660
Jungling is America's oldest brewery and began right where I am from in Pottsville, Pennsylvania.
我查过了。

1987
01:27:49.240 --> 01:27:50.480
I looked it up.
它成立于1829年。

1988
01:27:50.740 --> 01:27:52.720
It was founded in 1829.
他们至今仍在酿酒。

1989
01:27:53.660 --> 01:27:55.660
They are still brewing today.
梅根想向道具组大声致谢，因为他们再一次展现了对细节的极致关注，

1990
01:27:56.300 --> 01:28:00.260
Megan would like to give a big shout-out to the props department for, yet again,
特意选用了宾夕法尼亚州东北部许多家庭冰箱里肯定能找到的本地啤酒。

1991
01:28:00.360 --> 01:28:08.500
paying such close attention to detail by including a local beer that is most definitely found in many refrigerators in northeastern Pennsylvania.
干得漂亮，菲尔·谢伊。

1992
01:28:08.920 --> 01:28:10.540
Nicely done, Phil Shea.
确实。

1993
01:28:10.680 --> 01:28:11.180
I know.
我知道。

1994
01:28:11.720 --> 01:28:15.660
Megan would also like us to know, Angela, that if we ever come to Scranton,
安吉拉，梅根还想告诉我们，如果我们哪天去斯克兰顿，

1995
01:28:15.840 --> 01:28:21.280
the Jungling Brewery Tour is just about an hour away, and it's really great.
云岭酿酒厂之旅就在一小时车程外，真的很棒。

1996
01:28:21.940 --> 01:28:27.040
Maybe we can time that out, and we'll hit the What the Fluff Festival, and then go for a factory tour.
也许我们可以安排一下时间，先去参加“棉花糖节”，再去参观工厂。

1997
01:28:27.440 --> 01:28:28.240
I like it.
我喜欢这个主意。

1998
01:28:28.420 --> 01:28:28.680
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

1999
01:28:28.820 --> 01:28:30.680
Will I get through the What the Fluff Festival?
我能熬过那个“棉花糖节”吗？

2000
01:28:30.960 --> 01:28:31.620
I don't think you will.
我觉得你熬不过。

2001
01:28:31.700 --> 01:28:32.980
I think you're going to offend someone.
我觉得你会冒犯到别人。

2002
01:28:34.480 --> 01:28:39.500
Per Randy Cordray, the hot tub scene at 16 minutes, 46 seconds was already there.
据兰迪·科德雷所说，16分46秒处的按摩浴缸场景本来就在那儿。

2003
01:28:39.700 --> 01:28:41.360
We did not have to provide that.
我们不需要提供那个。

2004
01:28:41.920 --> 01:28:42.320
Hey!
嘿！

2005
01:28:42.560 --> 01:28:42.960
Hey there!
嘿，你好！

2006
01:28:42.960 --> 01:28:45.180
We just used those people's hot tub?
我们刚才用了那些人的按摩浴缸？

2007
01:28:45.300 --> 01:28:45.760
They did.
他们用了。

2008
01:28:46.180 --> 01:28:46.500
Okay.
好吧。

2009
01:28:47.780 --> 01:28:56.100
Well, in this hot tub scene, Michael is trying to get David to, like, give him ideas on how to deal with Saber,
在这个按摩浴缸的场景里，迈克尔想让大卫给他出出主意，好让他去应付赛博公司，

2010
01:28:56.200 --> 01:28:58.440
but David is thinking about something else.
但大卫在想别的事情。

2011
01:28:58.440 --> 01:29:02.080
He's thinking about a new company that he wants to start.
他在想自己想创办的一家新公司。

2012
01:29:02.980 --> 01:29:07.760
It is a shop vac that will suck up kids' toys.
那是一个能把孩子玩具吸进去的吸尘器。

2013
01:29:09.100 --> 01:29:11.100
Does it make that noise?
它会发出那种声音吗？

2014
01:29:11.320 --> 01:29:12.320
Michael wants to know.
迈克尔想知道。

2015
01:29:12.520 --> 01:29:13.880
Do the kids make that noise?
是孩子们会发出那种声音吗？

2016
01:29:13.980 --> 01:29:14.900
Who makes that noise?
谁会发出那种声音？

2017
01:29:14.900 --> 01:29:16.240
Where's the noise coming from?
那声音是从哪儿来的？

2018
01:29:16.560 --> 01:29:19.140
And he tells Michael it's called Suck It.
然后他告诉迈克尔，它叫“吸它”。

2019
01:29:19.740 --> 01:29:27.200
Writer Jen Salata told us that it was her mom that came up with the idea for Suck It.
编剧简·萨拉塔告诉我们，是她妈妈想出了“吸它”这个点子。

2020
01:29:27.480 --> 01:29:28.400
Come on.
得了吧。

2021
01:29:28.400 --> 01:29:31.180
Yes, Jen Salata's mom had this idea.
没错，简·萨拉塔的妈妈想到了这个点子。

2022
01:29:31.480 --> 01:29:35.020
She thought it would be a very fun way to teach kids how to pick up their toys.
她觉得这会是一种非常有趣的方式，能教孩子们如何收拾玩具。

2023
01:29:35.360 --> 01:29:36.680
That is adorable!
那太可爱了！

2024
01:29:37.240 --> 01:29:41.800
So when she needed an invention for David Wallace, she thought of her mom's idea.
所以当她需要为大卫·华莱士构思一个发明时，她想到了她妈妈的点子。

2025
01:29:42.200 --> 01:29:47.600
And she said she thought it was perfect because since he's been home with his kids more often,
她说她觉得这很完美，因为自从大卫更多时间待在家里陪孩子后，

2026
01:29:47.600 --> 01:29:50.520
he was probably frustrated with seeing their toys everywhere.
他可能对到处都是玩具感到很沮丧。

2027
01:29:50.520 --> 01:29:51.860
It felt very organic.
这感觉非常自然。

2028
01:29:52.200 --> 01:29:53.720
But how adorable is that?
但这难道不可爱吗？

2029
01:29:54.120 --> 01:29:54.940
So cute.
太可爱了。

2030
01:29:54.940 --> 01:29:58.940
Well, lady, I decided to Google, how do you suck up toys?
好吧，女士，我决定去谷歌搜一下，怎么把玩具吸起来？

2031
01:29:59.280 --> 01:29:59.740
Okay.
好的。

2032
01:30:00.140 --> 01:30:03.700
I found an article by Newsweek in March of 2022.
我找到了《新闻周刊》2022年3月的一篇文章。

2033
01:30:04.420 --> 01:30:09.440
It talked about a guy who built a real-life Suck It for Lego pieces.
文章讲了一个人制造了一个现实版的“吸它”，专门用来吸乐高积木。

2034
01:30:10.260 --> 01:30:10.860
Whoa.
哇。

2035
01:30:11.180 --> 01:30:12.200
How cool is that?
那有多酷啊？

2036
01:30:12.380 --> 01:30:16.360
His name is Matty Benedetto, and he posted the video.
他叫马蒂·贝内代托，他发布了那个视频。

2037
01:30:16.360 --> 01:30:21.920
It shows him using a custom vacuum to collect pieces of Lego on the floor.
视频展示了他用一个定制的吸尘器来收集地板上的乐高积木。

2038
01:30:22.200 --> 01:30:23.220
Here's the ad.
广告在这儿。

2039
01:30:23.940 --> 01:30:28.640
I invented the Lego Suck It, the world's first vacuum-powered Lego cleanup device
我发明了乐高“吸它”，这是世界上第一款真空动力的乐高清理装置，

2040
01:30:28.640 --> 01:30:31.860
that automatically sorts each brick by its individual size.
它能自动按尺寸对每块积木进行分类。

2041
01:30:32.120 --> 01:30:36.260
This vibrant cleaning accessory features a multistage chamber that magnetically separates
这款充满活力的清洁配件拥有一个多级腔室，可以通过磁力分离，

2042
01:30:36.260 --> 01:30:38.820
for quick organization of your favorite toy bricks.
让你心爱的玩具积木快速归位。

2043
01:30:39.080 --> 01:30:42.440
The high-suction vacuum will ensure every brick is sucked up from your floors
大功率真空吸尘器能确保每一块积木都从地板上被吸走，

2044
01:30:42.440 --> 01:30:44.540
and swiftly deposited into the clear tube
并迅速进入透明管中，

2045
01:30:44.540 --> 01:30:48.480
as each Lego piece cascades down and gets sorted into their different sizes.
随着每一块乐高积木落下，它们会被分拣成不同的大小。

2046
01:30:48.840 --> 01:30:51.720
So when things get messy, just suck it.
所以当家里乱糟糟的时候，就“吸它”吧。

2047
01:30:54.640 --> 01:30:55.080
Wow.
哇。

2048
01:30:55.400 --> 01:30:56.920
How many Legos have you stepped on?
你踩到过多少次乐高积木？

2049
01:30:57.040 --> 01:30:57.860
It's so painful.
那太疼了。

2050
01:30:58.180 --> 01:31:02.020
But how are you not sucking up everything on the floor?
但你怎么保证不会把地板上所有的东西都吸进去呢？

2051
01:31:02.240 --> 01:31:05.080
Like, how is there not also a goldfish in there, maybe?
比如，里面怎么可能没有金鱼呢，也许？

2052
01:31:05.360 --> 01:31:06.580
Oh, or a Cheerio.
哦，或者一个圆圈麦片。

2053
01:31:06.760 --> 01:31:07.180
Exactly.
没错。

2054
01:31:07.340 --> 01:31:07.600
Right.
对。

2055
01:31:07.740 --> 01:31:08.320
I don't know.
我不知道。

2056
01:31:08.420 --> 01:31:10.000
You have to have a clean Lego space.
你得有一个干净的乐高空间。

2057
01:31:10.200 --> 01:31:11.140
This is the thing.
关键就在这儿。

2058
01:31:11.240 --> 01:31:16.620
This is kind of the problem with David's invention, which is that, you know,
这就是大卫发明的问题所在，你知道的，

2059
01:31:16.620 --> 01:31:21.900
yes, you're going to suck up the toys, but how do you avoid sucking up everything else?
没错，你是会把玩具吸进去，但你怎么避免把其他东西也吸进去呢？

2060
01:31:22.260 --> 01:31:23.340
You just suck it.
你直接吸就行了。

2061
01:31:23.480 --> 01:31:24.360
You just suck it.
你直接吸就行了。

2062
01:31:24.440 --> 01:31:25.020
You just suck it.
你直接吸就行了。

2063
01:31:26.460 --> 01:31:29.040
Well, Michael's going to leave David Wallace's.
好吧，迈克尔要离开大卫·华莱士家了。

2064
01:31:29.140 --> 01:31:30.700
He's so defeated.
他看起来太受挫了。

2065
01:31:31.180 --> 01:31:36.320
He says very few things would make him say no to working with David Wallace, but suck it is one of them.
他说很少有事情能让他拒绝和大卫·华莱士共事，但“吸它”是其中之一。

2066
01:31:36.320 --> 01:31:50.400
And then I love this shot of Michael slowly pulling away, and David Wallace is sort of like lumbering down the driveway toward the car, like kind of like a zombie.
然后我喜欢迈克尔慢慢开车离开的那个镜头，大卫·华莱士像个僵尸一样在车道上蹒跚走向车子。

2067
01:31:50.600 --> 01:31:52.420
It reminded me of a zombie movie.
这让我想起了僵尸电影。

2068
01:31:53.560 --> 01:32:00.180
Jen Salata said this shot was John's idea, and I think it is some foreshadowing for A Quiet Place.
简·萨拉塔说这个镜头是约翰的主意，我觉得这算是《寂静之地》的伏笔。

2069
01:32:00.320 --> 01:32:00.920
Oh, really?
哦，真的吗？

2070
01:32:01.060 --> 01:32:01.680
I really do.
我真的这么觉得。

2071
01:32:01.800 --> 01:32:03.340
It felt very horror movie to me.
我觉得这非常有恐怖片的感觉。

2072
01:32:03.660 --> 01:32:10.980
Right after this creepy goodbye with David Wallace would have come the final accounting scene in the runner that got deleted.
在这次令人毛骨悚然的告别之后，本该是那个被删减的副线剧情中会计部的最后一场戏。

2073
01:32:11.640 --> 01:32:13.420
Here's how it wraps up.
结局是这样的。

2074
01:32:13.860 --> 01:32:15.900
Accounting area, interior office.
会计部，办公室内部。

2075
01:32:16.200 --> 01:32:18.200
Angela's looking over Kevin's work.
安吉拉正在检查凯文的工作。

2076
01:32:18.420 --> 01:32:20.240
Angela, who did this?
安吉拉，这是谁做的？

2077
01:32:20.600 --> 01:32:21.780
Oscar shakes his head.
奥斯卡摇了摇头。

2078
01:32:22.420 --> 01:32:25.300
Kevin, it's possible I did it on my own.
凯文，有没有可能是我自己做的？

2079
01:32:25.600 --> 01:32:27.700
Angela, no, it's not.
安吉拉，不，不可能。

2080
01:32:28.020 --> 01:32:30.680
That is the one thing that is not possible.
那是唯一不可能的事情。

2081
01:32:30.940 --> 01:32:31.920
How did this happen?
这到底是怎么回事？

2082
01:32:32.540 --> 01:32:34.380
Kevin, I did it.
凯文，是我做的。

2083
01:32:34.820 --> 01:32:37.440
Angela sighs, confused, and walks away.
安吉拉困惑地叹了口气，走开了。

2084
01:32:37.640 --> 01:32:41.320
Just missing Todd, who approaches with Kevin's adding machine.
刚好错过了托德，他拿着凯文的加法机走过来。

2085
01:32:41.820 --> 01:32:42.780
Kevin looks nervous.
凯文看起来很紧张。

2086
01:32:43.420 --> 01:32:45.680
Todd, you left this on my desk.
托德，你把这个落在我的桌子上了。

2087
01:32:45.920 --> 01:32:46.960
Kevin, thanks.
凯文，谢谢。

2088
01:32:47.620 --> 01:32:49.860
Todd and Oscar ad-lib hellos.
托德和奥斯卡即兴打了招呼。

2089
01:32:50.100 --> 01:32:51.320
And Todd leaves.
然后托德离开了。

2090
01:32:51.320 --> 01:32:53.820
Kevin, don't tell Angela.
凯文，别告诉安吉拉。

2091
01:32:54.260 --> 01:32:55.720
Oscar, I won't.
奥斯卡，我不会的。

2092
01:32:56.120 --> 01:32:58.600
Kevin, I didn't just let him do the work.
凯文，我不仅仅是让他帮我做工作。

2093
01:32:58.720 --> 01:32:59.480
I learned stuff.
我学到了东西。

2094
01:32:59.700 --> 01:33:00.600
Please don't tell her.
求你别告诉她。

2095
01:33:00.940 --> 01:33:02.820
Oscar, I said I won't.
奥斯卡，我说过我不会的。

2096
01:33:03.260 --> 01:33:04.380
Kevin looks nervous.
凯文看起来很紧张。

2097
01:33:04.920 --> 01:33:05.980
Oscar looks up at him.
奥斯卡抬头看着他。

2098
01:33:06.380 --> 01:33:09.080
Oscar, he trained me when I started here.
奥斯卡，我刚来这里的时候是他训练我的。

2099
01:33:09.560 --> 01:33:10.620
Kevin, really?
凯文，真的吗？

2100
01:33:10.620 --> 01:33:12.040
Kelly, Oscar nods.
凯利，奥斯卡点了点头。

2101
01:33:12.160 --> 01:33:12.980
And Kevin smiles.
凯文笑了。

2102
01:33:13.740 --> 01:33:16.160
What a sweet ending to that story.
那个故事的结局真温馨。

2103
01:33:16.420 --> 01:33:16.800
I know.
我知道。

2104
01:33:16.820 --> 01:33:19.100
And who is Todd at Vance Refrigeration?
那万斯制冷公司的托德又是谁？

2105
01:33:19.380 --> 01:33:23.980
I mean, it kind of sounded like maybe Todd used to work at Dunder Mifflin.
我是说，听起来托德好像以前在邓德·米夫林工作过。

2106
01:33:24.160 --> 01:33:24.780
I don't know.
我不知道。

2107
01:33:24.780 --> 01:33:26.400
I feel like Todd is training people.
我觉得托德是在培训新人。

2108
01:33:27.300 --> 01:33:27.540
Yeah.
是啊。

2109
01:33:28.180 --> 01:33:30.040
Anyway, I thought that was sort of sweet.
总之，我觉得那段挺温馨的。

2110
01:33:30.340 --> 01:33:35.280
Well, speaking of sweet, something we haven't talked about is that cutie pie scene at reception
说到温馨，我们还没聊到前台那个可爱的场景呢

2111
01:33:35.280 --> 01:33:40.120
with Andy and Erin, where they're both not asking each other out on a date.
安迪和艾琳都在那儿，却谁也没约对方出去。

2112
01:33:40.660 --> 01:33:42.100
It's a totally adorable scene.
那场景真是太可爱了。

2113
01:33:42.420 --> 01:33:48.180
But if I'm being honest, I was very distracted by the fact that Pam's watercolor is back on the wall.
但老实说，帕姆的水彩画又挂回墙上了，这让我很分心。

2114
01:33:48.280 --> 01:33:49.000
It's all I could see.
我满眼看到的都是它。

2115
01:33:49.800 --> 01:33:50.780
It's all I could see.
我满眼看到的都是它。

2116
01:33:50.860 --> 01:33:51.840
I was just so delighted.
我真是太高兴了。

2117
01:33:52.760 --> 01:33:55.180
Michael's going to return, and he's going to make a toaSt.
迈克尔要回来了，他要发表祝酒词。

2118
01:33:55.400 --> 01:33:57.000
He's brought a lot of orange juice.
他带了很多橙汁。

2119
01:33:57.040 --> 01:33:59.660
I guess in honor of Tallahassee.
我想是为了纪念塔拉哈西。

2120
01:33:59.940 --> 01:34:00.380
Florida.
佛罗里达。

2121
01:34:00.760 --> 01:34:00.980
Yeah.
是啊。

2122
01:34:01.280 --> 01:34:01.720
Oranges.
橙子。

2123
01:34:02.220 --> 01:34:03.400
He makes his toaSt.
他发表了祝酒词。

2124
01:34:03.400 --> 01:34:04.580
It's not really a toaSt.
那其实算不上祝酒词。

2125
01:34:04.760 --> 01:34:08.000
But everyone takes a big swig, and they're like, ugh.
但每个人都喝了一大口，然后都露出了那种表情，呃。

2126
01:34:08.600 --> 01:34:13.680
Yeah, I mean, have you ever had orange juice out of, like, an aluminum container?
是啊，我是说，你有没有试过用铝制容器喝橙汁？

2127
01:34:14.040 --> 01:34:14.820
A little tangy.
有点酸涩。

2128
01:34:15.360 --> 01:34:18.960
Yeah, I mean, it goes right into your teeth, just like they talk about.
是啊，就像人们说的那样，那种感觉直接渗进牙齿里。

2129
01:34:19.200 --> 01:34:20.160
But you know what?
但你知道吗？

2130
01:34:20.160 --> 01:34:24.540
I don't think that a lot of people are putting their orange juice into metal containers.
我觉得没多少人会把橙汁装进金属容器里。

2131
01:34:24.540 --> 01:34:27.000
You know, it's really meant to be a water vessel.
你知道的，那本来是用来装水的容器。

2132
01:34:27.440 --> 01:34:28.900
I like that you said vessel.
我喜欢你用的那个词，容器。

2133
01:34:29.160 --> 01:34:29.680
Thank you.
谢谢。

2134
01:34:29.920 --> 01:34:31.180
It's a water vessel.
那是装水的容器。

2135
01:34:31.340 --> 01:34:31.920
It is.
确实。

2136
01:34:32.160 --> 01:34:34.960
Well, I guess this toast means that Michael is going to stay.
好吧，我想这次祝酒意味着迈克尔要留下来了。

2137
01:34:35.160 --> 01:34:36.720
He's going to stick it out at Saber.
他要在赛博公司坚持下去。

2138
01:34:36.900 --> 01:34:37.380
He is.
是的。

2139
01:34:37.380 --> 01:34:41.580
He's going to, I guess, have to abide by all these new rules that he hates.
我想他得遵守所有那些他讨厌的新规定了。

2140
01:34:41.760 --> 01:34:43.940
No YouTube for Michael at work?
迈克尔在公司不能看YouTube了？

2141
01:34:44.180 --> 01:34:45.580
I don't know how that's going to go.
我不知道这会变成什么样。

2142
01:34:45.820 --> 01:34:50.360
The episode ends with David Wallace and his son jamming out to a song that he's written
剧集结尾是戴维·华莱士和他儿子一起演奏他写的一首歌

2143
01:34:50.360 --> 01:34:51.200
called Suck It.
歌名叫《吸干它》。

2144
01:34:51.360 --> 01:34:52.520
Let's listen to it.
我们来听听看。

2145
01:35:29.070 --> 01:35:30.910
Oh, and Daniel's on the druMs.
哦，丹尼尔在打鼓。

2146
01:35:30.950 --> 01:35:31.730
I know, man.
我知道，伙计。

2147
01:35:32.270 --> 01:35:36.410
We got a fan question from Donna in Newport, New Virginia.
我们收到了来自新弗吉尼亚州纽波特的唐娜发来的粉丝提问。

2148
01:35:36.830 --> 01:35:41.230
Please tell us everything about the Suck It song at the end of the episode,
请告诉我们关于剧集结尾那首《吸干它》的一切，

2149
01:35:41.230 --> 01:35:43.110
How Much Was Improvised.
有多少是即兴创作的。

2150
01:35:43.490 --> 01:35:49.970
I will tell you, Donna, the lyrics to Suck It were written by Warren and Halstead.
唐娜，我来告诉你，《吸干它》的歌词是沃伦和霍尔斯特德写的。

2151
01:35:50.130 --> 01:35:53.370
These guys really were our go-to guys for song lyrics.
这些人确实是我们歌词的首选。

2152
01:35:54.030 --> 01:35:58.110
And then I reached out to Andy Buckley, who plays David Wallace.
然后我联系了饰演大卫·华莱士的安迪·巴克利。

2153
01:35:58.430 --> 01:36:03.450
He said that Warren and Halstead faxed him the lyrics the night before.
他说沃伦和霍尔斯特德在前一天晚上把歌词传真给了他。

2154
01:36:04.050 --> 01:36:05.530
Old tech alert, Angela.
老古董警报，安吉拉。

2155
01:36:05.810 --> 01:36:06.210
Fax.
传真。

2156
01:36:06.390 --> 01:36:06.690
Yeah.
没错。

2157
01:36:06.930 --> 01:36:07.930
They faxed them.
他们传真过来的。

2158
01:36:08.330 --> 01:36:09.830
He went over them that night.
他那天晚上研究了一下。

2159
01:36:09.970 --> 01:36:11.990
He went over them at lunch the next day.
第二天午饭时他又研究了一下。

2160
01:36:12.210 --> 01:36:15.330
That's when he was able to kind of fiddle around on a keyboard.
就在那时，他得以在键盘上摆弄了一番。

2161
01:36:15.730 --> 01:36:20.090
Andy said he only knew about three chords on a keyboard.
安迪说他只会弹键盘上的三个和弦。

2162
01:36:20.430 --> 01:36:21.810
But he did his beSt.
但他尽力了。

2163
01:36:22.030 --> 01:36:24.730
They did not shoot this until the very end of the day.
他们直到当天最后才拍摄这一幕。

2164
01:36:24.830 --> 01:36:27.870
They only had three minutes before they went into overtime.
在进入加班时间前，他们只有三分钟时间。

2165
01:36:27.870 --> 01:36:29.370
Three minutes.
三分钟。

2166
01:36:29.650 --> 01:36:29.950
Yes.
是的。

2167
01:36:30.210 --> 01:36:31.350
No messing up.
不能搞砸。

2168
01:36:31.510 --> 01:36:31.990
Yes.
没错。

2169
01:36:32.210 --> 01:36:35.990
He said it all came together when Owen started playing the druMs.
他说当欧文开始打鼓时，一切都顺了。

2170
01:36:36.250 --> 01:36:37.730
It was kind of a magic moment.
那真是一个神奇的时刻。

2171
01:36:38.150 --> 01:36:39.870
They got it in three takes.
他们拍了三条就过了。

2172
01:36:40.270 --> 01:36:43.090
He said that the whole shoot day was a total blaSt.
他说整个拍摄日都非常开心。

2173
01:36:43.250 --> 01:36:44.850
He loved John as a director.
他很喜欢约翰作为导演的表现。

2174
01:36:45.170 --> 01:36:47.990
He said John just had tons of positive energy.
他说约翰总是充满正能量。

2175
01:36:48.590 --> 01:36:51.110
And that John and Jen Salata worked so well together.
而且约翰和简·萨拉塔合作得非常默契。

2176
01:36:51.350 --> 01:36:54.590
He sort of remembered that someone would do a take,
他隐约记得有人拍完一条后，

2177
01:36:54.590 --> 01:36:56.770
and then you'd see them kind of huddle and giggle,
你会看到他们聚在一起窃窃私语，

2178
01:36:56.950 --> 01:36:58.950
and then they would come over with some great idea.
然后他们就会带着很棒的点子走过来。

2179
01:36:59.110 --> 01:37:00.190
That's my memory, too.
这也是我的记忆。

2180
01:37:00.390 --> 01:37:00.650
Same.
我也是。

2181
01:37:01.070 --> 01:37:02.850
Andy said that inevitably,
安迪说不可避免的是，

2182
01:37:03.470 --> 01:37:07.850
if he is walking through an airport or some public place,
如果他走在机场或什么公共场所，

2183
01:37:08.070 --> 01:37:10.370
there is always someone who yells out,
总会有人大喊，

2184
01:37:10.510 --> 01:37:11.150
suck it!
去你的！

2185
01:37:13.150 --> 01:37:20.940
He said that he and Zach Woods are now on an HBO comedy called Avenue 5,
他说他和扎克·伍兹现在正在拍一部HBO的喜剧叫《第五大道》，

2186
01:37:20.980 --> 01:37:22.760
and it films in London.
在伦敦拍摄。

2187
01:37:23.000 --> 01:37:23.600
Amazing.
太棒了。

2188
01:37:23.600 --> 01:37:26.980
He said they were in London for four months before the pandemic
他说在疫情前，也就是2019年夏天，

2189
01:37:26.980 --> 01:37:28.940
in the summer of 2019,
他们在伦敦待了四个月，

2190
01:37:28.940 --> 01:37:32.620
and they just went back this past fall in 2021.
去年秋天，也就是2021年，他们刚回去过。

2191
01:37:33.500 --> 01:37:38.300
He said that the difference between people stopping the two of them
他说人们拦住他们两个人的频率，

2192
01:37:38.300 --> 01:37:41.880
before the pandemic and after the pandemic is striking.
在疫情前后有着惊人的差异。

2193
01:37:42.380 --> 01:37:46.740
He said clearly a lot of people binged the office during the pandemic.
他说很明显很多人在疫情期间刷了《办公室》。

2194
01:37:47.000 --> 01:37:49.600
He's getting way more suckets lately.
他最近收到的“去你的”多多了。

2195
01:37:50.540 --> 01:37:52.300
Well, I hope that's a good thing.
嗯，我希望这是件好事。

2196
01:37:52.500 --> 01:37:53.180
I think so.
我想是的。

2197
01:37:53.800 --> 01:37:55.620
Well, that is the Saber episode.
好了，这就是“赛博”那一集。

2198
01:37:55.900 --> 01:37:57.160
Well, before we go,
嗯，在结束之前，

2199
01:37:57.380 --> 01:38:00.900
I thought we could end this episode with a few final thoughts from John
我想我们可以用约翰的一些最后感想来结束这一集，

2200
01:38:00.900 --> 01:38:04.100
about what directing this episode meant to him.
关于执导这一集对他意味着什么。

2201
01:38:04.940 --> 01:38:07.660
This show was my film school.
这部剧就是我的电影学院。

2202
01:38:07.920 --> 01:38:10.980
I mean, I was a waiter before I got the show.
我是说，在接这部剧之前，我只是个服务员。

2203
01:38:11.160 --> 01:38:13.560
I had zero experience in this business,
我在这行里毫无经验，

2204
01:38:13.920 --> 01:38:17.360
in anything, acting, writing, directing, producing, any of that stuff.
什么经验都没有，表演、编剧、导演、制片，这些统统没有。

2205
01:38:17.360 --> 01:38:20.340
And there it was right in front of me.
而它就这样直接出现在我面前。

2206
01:38:20.580 --> 01:38:29.360
I was somehow lucky enough to drop right into the most immense pool of talent that I could ever ask for.
我当时真是幸运，一头扎进了我所能想象到的最顶尖的人才库里。

2207
01:38:29.360 --> 01:38:35.260
From that insane group of people that we had as our writers,
从我们那群疯狂的编剧团队开始，

2208
01:38:35.260 --> 01:38:39.680
a group of people that hadn't been assembled like that in a long, long time, I guess.
我想，那样的阵容已经很久很久没有出现过了。

2209
01:38:39.860 --> 01:38:42.400
And, you know, in a lot of ways, probably hasn't been assembled since.
而且，你知道，在很多方面，可能从那以后就再也没出现过这样的组合了。

2210
01:38:42.400 --> 01:38:49.000
So, from writing, I got to understand what it meant to not only write well,
所以，从编剧方面，我不仅学会了什么是写出好剧本，

2211
01:38:49.240 --> 01:38:53.560
but to have fun with it and to have this drive to do something great.
还学会了如何从中寻找乐趣，以及那种追求卓越的动力。

2212
01:38:53.560 --> 01:38:59.000
I remember going up to the writers' rooms every now and again if I had a break between scenes or something
我记得偶尔在两场戏的间隙，我会去编剧室转转，

2213
01:38:59.000 --> 01:39:02.640
and, you know, watch them all come up with an idea.
看着他们所有人一起构思点子。

2214
01:39:02.920 --> 01:39:06.620
And the idea was almost like you could visualize a balloon in the air.
那个过程就像你能看见一个气球在空中飘浮一样。

2215
01:39:06.880 --> 01:39:10.080
And 12 to 14 people were making sure it didn't touch the ground before
十二到十四个人确保它不会落地，直到

2216
01:39:10.080 --> 01:39:14.200
they had investigated every single possibility of how to make it work
他们穷尽了所有让它成行的可能性，

2217
01:39:14.200 --> 01:39:17.740
and how to make a joke work or an idea work.
以及如何让一个笑话或一个点子奏效。

2218
01:39:18.220 --> 01:39:20.640
And even if it didn't end up working,
即使最后没能用上，

2219
01:39:20.800 --> 01:39:22.740
they still put it on a different board that was like,
他们也会把它写在另一块板子上，意思是，

2220
01:39:22.740 --> 01:39:25.540
well, it's not working today, but maybe it'll work again on another day.
好吧，今天行不通，但也许改天就能用了。

2221
01:39:25.740 --> 01:39:30.360
And if it did work, they all worked together to try to figure out
如果确实奏效了，他们会齐心协力去琢磨

2222
01:39:30.360 --> 01:39:33.160
how it would best be used in this show.
如何把它在剧中发挥到极致。

2223
01:39:33.600 --> 01:39:38.660
So, writing-wise, there I was, front row seats in the greatest room I could be.
所以，在编剧方面，我当时就像坐在了最棒的教室里的前排座位上。

2224
01:39:39.160 --> 01:39:43.040
Visually, I had no idea what even a camera was.
在视觉方面，我甚至连摄像机是什么都不知道。

2225
01:39:43.140 --> 01:39:45.540
I didn't know what the difference between lenses were.
我不知道镜头之间有什么区别。

2226
01:39:45.800 --> 01:39:48.740
I didn't know what type of cameras did what.
我不知道什么类型的摄像机是干什么用的。

2227
01:39:49.000 --> 01:39:52.140
But I remember there again, the office was shot.
但我记得当时《办公室》的拍摄方式，

2228
01:39:52.740 --> 01:39:53.440
Single camera.
单机位拍摄。

2229
01:39:53.660 --> 01:39:57.040
It was one of the first shows to be done like that in a long time.
那是很久以来第一部采用这种方式拍摄的剧集之一。

2230
01:39:57.260 --> 01:40:00.060
And so, we were shooting the show like a movie.
所以，我们当时就像拍电影一样在拍这部剧。

2231
01:40:00.380 --> 01:40:06.120
So, I got to pick the brain of Randall and Matt and all these hugely talented people
我有机会向兰德尔、马特以及所有这些才华横溢的人请教，

2232
01:40:06.120 --> 01:40:11.820
who would explain to me everything from lenses to the aesthetic and lighting
他们会向我解释一切，从镜头到美学和灯光，

2233
01:40:11.820 --> 01:40:14.880
and all these different things that I had absolutely no idea about.
以及所有我完全一窍不通的事情。

2234
01:40:14.880 --> 01:40:20.380
After directing Saber, I used to hang out with Dave Rogers and watch him cut all these different episodes.
执导完《Saber》那一集后，我经常和戴夫·罗杰斯待在一起，看他剪辑各种剧集。

2235
01:40:20.560 --> 01:40:24.760
I'd pop in and see how he was making all these scenes work, how he was rearranging story,
我会顺道去看看他是如何让这些场景变得生动，如何重组故事，

2236
01:40:24.980 --> 01:40:29.960
how he was taking air out of a moment to make it work so much stronger.
如何通过删减冗余来让一个瞬间变得更有张力。

2237
01:40:30.960 --> 01:40:37.800
So, really, at the end of the day, I certainly would not be writing and directing at all if it wasn't for this show.
所以，说到底，如果不是因为这部剧，我肯定根本不会从事编剧和导演工作。

2238
01:40:37.800 --> 01:40:40.280
I just, I wouldn't have had the confidence to do it.
我只是，我当时根本没有信心去做这些。

2239
01:40:40.440 --> 01:40:46.100
So, I guess what I'm trying to say is I owe absolutely everything to this show.
所以，我想说的是，我的一切都归功于这部剧。

2240
01:40:46.740 --> 01:40:50.540
Directing this episode was so very special.
执导这一集对我来说非常特别。

2241
01:40:50.700 --> 01:40:53.420
And I will truly never forget it.
我真的永远不会忘记。

2242
01:40:53.920 --> 01:40:55.540
Thank you for having me on the show.
谢谢你们邀请我参加节目。

2243
01:40:55.920 --> 01:40:59.660
I was so excited to come back and have me back again.
能回来参加节目，我感到非常兴奋。

2244
01:41:00.240 --> 01:41:01.940
But again, like I said, slow down.
但再说一次，就像我说的，慢一点。

2245
01:41:02.140 --> 01:41:03.260
We need more of this podcaSt.
我们需要更多的播客内容。

2246
01:41:03.500 --> 01:41:03.660
Please.
拜托了。

2247
01:41:04.400 --> 01:41:06.260
John Krasinski, we love you.
约翰·卡拉辛斯基，我们爱你。

2248
01:41:06.500 --> 01:41:07.900
Love you so much, John.
非常爱你，约翰。

2249
01:41:08.080 --> 01:41:09.920
That was so generous of you.
你真是太慷慨了。

2250
01:41:10.120 --> 01:41:11.500
You shared so much with us.
你和我们分享了这么多。

2251
01:41:11.660 --> 01:41:13.880
And I just loved hearing your voice.
我真的很喜欢听你的声音。

2252
01:41:14.180 --> 01:41:14.700
Same.
我也是。

2253
01:41:15.160 --> 01:41:21.160
Also want to give a big thank you to Yaki Margulies for that amazing Beach Boys parody.
还要特别感谢 Yaki Margulies 带来的那段精彩的“海滩男孩”模仿秀。

2254
01:41:21.560 --> 01:41:26.980
You can find him on Twitter and Instagram at Yaki Margulies.
你可以在 Twitter 和 Instagram 上关注他，账号是 Yaki Margulies。

2255
01:41:27.200 --> 01:41:30.740
And you can listen to his music online anywhere that you get your music,
你可以在任何音乐平台上在线收听他的音乐，

2256
01:41:30.740 --> 01:41:33.420
like Spotify, Apple Music, iTunes, etc.
比如 Spotify、Apple Music、iTunes 等等。

2257
01:41:34.060 --> 01:41:39.580
I also want to say thank you to Joya Balfour for sharing with us what NBC Digital was up to.
我还要感谢 Joya Balfour 与我们分享 NBC Digital 的近况。

2258
01:41:39.900 --> 01:41:43.460
And to Randy Cordray and Warren Lieberstein and Halstead Sullivan.
还要感谢 Randy Cordray、Warren Lieberstein 和 Halstead Sullivan。

2259
01:41:43.780 --> 01:41:45.160
Thank you, everyone.
谢谢大家。

2260
01:41:45.520 --> 01:41:47.740
We will see you next week.
我们下周见。

2261
01:41:48.080 --> 01:41:50.440
I hope you enjoy a fluffernutter this week.
希望你们这周能享用一份棉花糖花生酱三明治。

2262
01:41:50.720 --> 01:41:51.900
I'm going to have one.
我打算吃一个。

2263
01:41:52.300 --> 01:41:53.120
Please do.
请务必尝尝。

2264
01:41:53.480 --> 01:41:54.160
I will.
我会的。

2265
01:41:54.420 --> 01:41:54.660
Okay.
好的。

2266
01:41:54.880 --> 01:41:55.900
Oh, on gluten-free bread.
哦，用无麸质面包做。

2267
01:41:56.380 --> 01:41:56.740
Yay.
耶。

2268
01:42:02.500 --> 01:42:04.460
Thank you for listening to Office Ladies.
感谢收听《办公室女士》。

2269
01:42:04.740 --> 01:42:08.660
